{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high vs realized levels (often by ~5–10 EUR, occasionally >10). Apply a downward level shift to the whole path, larger when recent history is elevated.",
    "Regime-change/turning-point miss: after a local peak in history, the model keeps projecting sideways-to-higher prices instead of the subsequent sell-off. If the last 1–2 weeks show a topping pattern (lower highs / loss of momentum), force a bearish drift for the next 10–20 days.",
    "Insufficient downside momentum: realized paths show sustained declines; forecasts revert upward too quickly. Reduce mean-reversion speed upward; allow negative trend persistence (carry recent negative returns forward).",
    "Horizon-dependent error: the overprediction grows with horizon (smallest in days 1–3, larger in days ~7–30). Use a horizon-increasing downward correction (e.g., subtract more as lead time increases).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: when history contains a spike/run-up, the model extrapolates it; reality mean-reverts sharply lower. Add a ‘post-spike’ penalty: if last-30d range is wide / recent volatility high, damp forecast levels and slope (cap upside, steepen early downside).",
    "Volatility mismatch: forecast paths are too smooth and too high; realized has sharper drawdowns. Increase downside tail adjustment (wider bands or explicit negative shock term) when recent realized volatility is rising."
  ]
}
